    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Given:\n- The least common multiple (LCM) of two integers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is 36.\n- The greatest common divisor (GCD) of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is 6.\n\nWe need to find the product of the two numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).\n\nFirst, we use the relationship between the LCM and GCD of two numbers:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(a, b) \\times \\text{GCD}(a, b) = a \\times b\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[\n36 \\times 6 = a \\times b\n\\]\n\\[\n216 = a \\times b\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the factors of 216 that can be the product of two integers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) with a GCD of 6. We start by finding the prime factorization of 216:\n\\[\n216 = 2^3 \\times 3^3\n\\]\n\nGiven that 6 is the GCD, we can express \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) in terms of their prime factors:\n\\[\na = 2^x \\times 3^y \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = 2^z \\times 3^w\n\\]\nwhere \\(x, y, z, w\\) are non-negative integers and \\(\\text{GCD}(a, b) = 6\\). This implies:\n\\[\nx \\leq 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y \\leq 1\n\\]\n\\[\nz \\leq 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad w \\leq 1\n\\]\n\nAdditionally, the product \\(a \\times b\\) must be 216:\n\\[\na \\times b = (2^x \\times 3^y) \\times (2^z \\times 3^w) = 2^{x+z} \\times 3^{y+w}\n\\]\nSetting this equal to 216, we have:\n\\[\n2^{x+z} \\times 3^{y+w} = 2^3 \\times 3^3\n\\]\n\nBy comparing the exponents:\n\\[\nx + z = 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y + w = 3\n\\]\n\nWe need to find pairs \\((x, z)\\) and \\((y, w)\\) that satisfy both equations and are consistent with \\(x, y, z, w \\leq 1\\).\n\nThe possible pairs for \\((x, z)\\) are:\n\\[\n(x, z) = (0, 3), (1, 2), (2, 1), (3, 0)\n\\]\n\nThe possible pairs for \\((y, w)\\) are:\n\\[\n(y, w) = (0, 3), (1, 2), (2, 1), (3, 0)\n\\]\n\nWe now check which pairs satisfy both equations:\n1. \\((x, z) = (0, 3)\\) and \\((y, w) = (0, 3)\\):\n   \\[\n   a = 2^0 \\times 3^3 = 27, \\quad b = 2^0 \\times 3^3 = 27 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad a = b = 27\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a \\times b = 27 \\times 27 = 729 \\quad \\text{(not 216)}\n   \\]\n\n2. \\((x, z) = (1, 2)\\) and \\((y, w) = (1, 2)\\):\n   \\[\n   a = 2^1 \\times 3^2 = 36, \\quad b = 2^1 \\times 3^2 = 36 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad a = b = 36\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a \\times b = 36 \\times 36 = 1296 \\quad \\text{(not 216)}\n   \\]\n\n3. \\((x, z) = (2, 1)\\) and \\((y, w) = (2, 1)\\):\n   \\[\n   a = 2^2 \\times 3^1 = 18, \\quad b = 2^2 \\times 3^1 = 18 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad a = b = 18\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a \\times b = 18 \\times 18 = 324 \\quad \\text{(not 216)}\n   \\]\n\n4. \\((x, z) = (3, 0)\\) and \\((y, w) = (3, 0)\\):\n   \\[\n   a = 2^3 \\times 3^0 = 8, \\quad b = 2^3 \\times 3^0 = 8 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad a = b = 8\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a \\times b = 8 \\times 8 = 64 \\quad \\text{(not 216)}\n   \\]\n\nNone of the above pairs yield the correct product of 216. Therefore, we re-evaluate our approach and find:\n\\[\na = 12 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = 18\n\\]\n\\[\na \\times b = 12 \\times 18 = 216\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the two numbers is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{216}\n\\]",
